module gunbc.scm.authoring

// THE AUTHORING VERB: how a user's edit becomes a stated requirement in the proposal they are
// preparing. This is the `add` half of the SCM surface. The read-side verbs already exist
// (gunbc.scm.checkout `checkout`, and the commit lookups), and this module is written to read as
// their sibling rather than as a second shape for the same layer.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT: it never touches persistence, and it is deliberately not a verb over a store.
// Its whole signature is (Proposal, Requirement) -> Proposal over an algebra that already exists on
// main, so the only thing it constrains about a future repository store is the shape of Proposal --
// which the store did not get to choose either. A verb that has to decide WHAT DURABLY HAPPENED
// (commit, and anything reporting a published outcome) does bake in assumptions its store must then
// satisfy, and none of those are here.

import std.types { Bool, List, String }
import v2.std.node { Symbol }
import gunbc.scm.proposal {
  Requirement,
  RequireBinding,
  RequireBindingAbsent,
  Proposal,
  requirement_role,
}

// THE ROLE-CONFLICT RULE IS DERIVED, AND IT LANDS OPPOSITE TO THE INTEGRATION'S -- WHICH IS THE POINT.
//
// gunbc.scm.role_requirement_integration `apply_requirement` states its rule in the strongest terms available: "ONLY THE
// FIRST AUTHORING RESOLVES THE ROLE. A later competing authoring is recorded as an alternative and
// must NOT overwrite it: silently taking the last writer is the fabricated winner this outcome type
// exists to refuse." Read naively, that settles this module too, and `add` would have to refuse the
// second add of a role -- or record it as a contested alternative.
//
// It does not settle it, because the two operations are asked DIFFERENT QUESTIONS about different
// populations, and the merge rule's own justification is what shows this:
//
//   MERGE combines proposals from SEVERAL AUTHORS. Two requirements for one role are two PARTIES
//   who each believe theirs is right, and neither authored in knowledge of the other. There is no
//   fact of the matter about which is later in any sense that means anything -- "last" is an
//   artifact of the order the merge happened to fold them. Picking one is fabricating a winner.
//
//   AUTHORING revises ONE author's OWN pending proposal, in the order THEY performed it. The second
//   add is not a competing party; it is the same party saying "no, this". Here "later" is not a fold
//   artifact -- it is the author's own sequence of intent, which is the only thing the operation has
//   to go on.
//
// So the merge rule is not overridden, it does not reach: its premise is a contest between authors,
// and an author has no contest with themselves. The mechanical tell is that merge's rule is stated
// over a LIST OF PROPOSALS whose order it explicitly refuses to privilege, while this is stated over
// ONE proposal being revised in place.
//
// AND THE ALTERNATIVE IS UNUSABLE, WHICH IS EVIDENCE THE DERIVATION IS RIGHT RATHER THAN CONVENIENT.
// If `add` refused or recorded an alternative on a repeated role, then the ordinary loop -- edit the
// file, add, notice a mistake, edit again, add -- would refuse on the second add, and the only way
// to correct a staged mistake would be to discard the proposal. A verb whose sole response to
// correction is "start over" has not modelled authoring; it has modelled a ledger.
//
// WHAT IS PRESERVED FROM merge's RULE: nothing is silently discarded ACROSS AUTHORS. This function
// produces one proposal per author, and when two authors' proposals meet, merge still sees exactly
// two competing requirements and still refuses to pick between them. Replacing within a draft does
// not weaken that; it is upstream of it.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO OUTCOME COPRODUCT HERE, unlike checkout: checkout can genuinely refuse -- an
// object may be missing from the store -- and its refusal arm is the discriminating half of its
// claim. This operation has no reachable refusal: any role may be authored, any requirement may
// replace any other for that role, and there is no store to consult. Minting an outcome type whose
// refusal arm can never be produced would be the permanently-green decoration DESIGN section 4b
// rules worse than absent, so the function is total and says so.
fn requirement_replaces_role(existing: Requirement, incoming: Requirement) -> Bool {
  (requirement_role(r: existing) as String) == (requirement_role(r: incoming) as String)
}

// The requirements this role's authoring must survive: every requirement the proposal already
// states EXCEPT the one this authoring supersedes. Order is otherwise preserved, so a proposal reads
// in the order its roles were first authored rather than being reshuffled by an unrelated revision.
fn requirements_without_role(
  requirements: List<Requirement>,
  incoming: Requirement,
) -> List<Requirement> {
  filter(requirements, r => !requirement_replaces_role(existing: r, incoming: incoming))
}

// AUTHOR ONE REQUIREMENT INTO A PENDING PROPOSAL. This is `gunbc add` for a present binding and
// `gunbc rm` for a stated absence -- ONE function, because they are one operation over the
// Requirement coproduct and forking them would mint two shapes for one concept. Which verb a user
// typed is a surface fact; what the proposal records is a stated requirement either way.
fn author_requirement(proposal: Proposal, requirement: Requirement) -> Proposal {
  Proposal {
    requirements: concat(
      requirements_without_role(requirements: proposal.requirements, incoming: requirement),
      [requirement],
    ),
    dependencies: proposal.dependencies,
  }
}

// Whether this proposal currently states anything about a role. This is what a status surface asks
// -- it is NOT "does the target have this binding", which is a question about a commit and belongs
// to the read verbs.
fn proposal_states_role(proposal: Proposal, role: Symbol) -> Bool {
  fold(proposal.requirements, init: false, f: fn(seen, r) {
    if seen { true } else { (requirement_role(r: r) as String) == (role as String) }
  })
}

fn empty_proposal() -> Proposal {
  Proposal { requirements: [], dependencies: [] }
}
